  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "The analysis is limited to structural and spatial distribution factors, omitting behavioral variations and actual travel patterns of the refugee population.",
      "what": "This study measures spatial accessibility to education, health, green spaces, and religious facilities for refugees in Konya, Türkiye, using the Three-Step Floating Catchment Area (3SFCA) method. By analyzing the spatial distribution of these facilities relative to refugee settlements, the research calculates accessibility scores, Lorenz curves, and Gini coefficients to isolate structural inequalities from behavioral differences. The analysis reveals that male refugees have higher accessibility scores across all four facility types, with the most pronounced gender disparities occurring in access to healthcare and green spaces.",
      "why": "It provides empirical evidence quantifying how the physical distribution of urban services and refugee housing creates baseline, gender-based spatial inequalities, showing that female refugees face higher within-group inequality and compound spatial disadvantages."
    }
